<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pemba, Mozambique</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Porto Amélia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pemba, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Amélia under Portuguese rule, is a port city on Pemba Bay, one of the largest natural harbours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-delgado">Cabo Delgado</a> province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. It was founded in 1904 by the Niassa Company, which moved its headquarters there from Ibo, and named after Queen Amélia of Portugal. It was renamed after Mozambique's independence in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pemba-mozambique.jpg" alt="Pemba, Mozambique" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ton Rulkens from Mozambique · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wimbe_Beach_2_(4871485865).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
